.TH HOLOD 8 2026-10-10 "holo 0.9.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
holod \- Holo routing daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B holod
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.br
.B holod
.BR \-h " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B holod
is the Holo routing daemon.
It implements BGP, IS\-IS, OSPF and RIP for routing, LDP for MPLS label
distribution, BFD for fast failure detection and VRRP for first hop
redundancy.
Routes learned by these protocols are collected in a routing table,
which selects the best route to each destination and installs it in the
kernel.
Each protocol can be disabled at build time.
.PP
Everything
.B holod
does is configured through YANG data models.
The running configuration starts out empty and is not read from disk.
Northbound clients such as
.B holo\-cli
create and change it through the gRPC or gNMI servers, and query
operational state the same way.
Each commit is recorded in the database, together with its author, and
clients can list and retrieve past transactions.
.PP
A separate configuration file, described in
.BR holod.toml (5),
holds the settings that must be known before the daemon starts, such as
the user and group to run as, logging and the northbound servers.
The file is read once at startup.
Changing it requires restarting
.BR holod .
.PP
.B holod
runs in the foreground and does not detach from the controlling
terminal.
It is meant to be supervised by a service manager such as
.BR systemd (1).
.SS Startup
On startup,
.B holod
performs the following steps, exiting with an error message on the
standard error if any of them fails:
.IP 1. 4
Read the configuration file.
If the file cannot be read, a warning is printed and the built\-in
defaults are used.
If the file cannot be parsed, or contains an unknown key, startup is
aborted.
.IP 2. 4
Verify that the effective user ID is 0.
.B holod
must be started as root.
.IP 3. 4
Set the file mode creation mask to 027, so that files created from then
on are accessible by the configured user and group only.
.IP 4. 4
Switch to the configured user and group, keeping
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ,
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
and
.B CAP_NET_RAW
in the permitted capability set.
These capabilities are raised into the effective set only around the
operations that require them.
.IP 5. 4
Take an exclusive lock on the
.B holod.lock
file in the state directory, which ensures that a single instance uses
that directory.
.IP 6. 4
Set up logging and open the database.
.IP 7. 4
Load the YANG modules and start the components that manage interfaces,
key chains, routing policies, system settings and the routing table.
.IP 8. 4
Start the enabled northbound servers.
.PP
Since the steps after the privilege drop run as the configured user,
the log directory, the state directory and the TLS certificate and key
must be accessible to that user or group.
.SS Northbound access
The gRPC server provides Holo's native northbound API, used by
.BR holo\-cli ,
on the
.B holod.sock
Unix socket in the state directory, and optionally on TCP addresses.
The gNMI server provides the OpenConfig gNMI service, on TCP addresses
only.
The TCP addresses of both servers are configured in
.BR holod.toml (5).
.PP
A Unix socket left behind by a previous run is removed before binding.
The new socket is created with mode 0660, so its file permissions decide
who may connect.
No password is requested, and requests are attributed to the user the
kernel reports for the peer.
With the default settings, members of the
.B holoadm
group have full administrative access to the router.
.PP
TLS is required on TCP addresses.
Clients on a TCP address must send the
.B username
and
.B password
metadata keys with every request, and are authenticated against the
users configured under
.IR /ietf\-system:system/authentication/user .
Requests carrying unknown users or wrong passwords are rejected with the
same error.
.PP
A server set to insecure mode in
.BR holod.toml (5)
serves its TCP addresses without TLS or authentication, giving anyone
who can reach them full administrative access to the router.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-config " file
Read the configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/holod.toml .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
The version includes the commit hash of the source tree when it was
available at build time.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Stop serving northbound clients, shut down the protocol instances and
exit.
.PP
No signal causes the configuration file to be read again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/holod.toml
Default configuration file.
See
.BR holod.toml (5).
.TP
.I /var/opt/holo
Default state directory.
.TP
.I /var/opt/holo/holod.lock
Default lock file preventing two instances from using the same state
directory.
.TP
.I /var/opt/holo/holo.db
Default database, holding the transaction history and the state
protocols keep across restarts, such as the boot counts OSPF and IS\-IS
use to protect authenticated packets against replay attacks.
.TP
.I /var/opt/holo/holod.sock
Default gRPC Unix socket.
.TP
.I /var/log/holo/holod.log
Default log file.
.TP
.IR /etc/holo/holo.pem ", " /etc/holo/holo.key
Default TLS certificate and key of the northbound servers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B holod
exits with status 0 after a shutdown requested by a signal, and with a
nonzero status if startup fails or a northbound server cannot serve its
address.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run with an alternate configuration file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# holod \-c /etc/holo/lab.toml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create the user, groups and directories expected by the default
configuration:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# groupadd \-r holo
# groupadd \-r holoadm
# useradd \-\-system \-\-shell /sbin/nologin \-\-home\-dir /var/opt/holo/ \e
    \-g holo holo
# mkdir /var/opt/holo /var/log/holo
# chown holo:holoadm /var/opt/holo /var/log/holo
# chmod 0750 /var/opt/holo /var/log/holo
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR holod.toml (5),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR systemd (1)
.PP
https://github.com/holo\-routing/holo
